\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee slips to 1-mth low, cbank hand seen   \b0\par\par\uc2 347 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2007\par 07:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 26 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee was pushed to its lowest in more than a month by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, though dollar purchases by oil companies and repatriation of profits by overseas investors also weighed, dealers said. The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 39.800/805 per dollar, its weakest since Oct. 23, and slipping from Friday's finish of 39.71/72. It had hit 39.16 earlier this month, its highest since March 1998.   \par \par \uc2 "The RBI seems to like getting the last word every day," said a dealer with a foreign bank, referring to the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that is widely suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily against the rupee in late deals.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI bought nearly $52 billion in the first nine months of 2007 in a bid to temper the rupee's ascent, and traders said it has been active in October and November.   \par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bucked a 2.1 percent rise in local stocks, with some foreign funds selling the local unit to repatriate profits before the end of the year, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 Foreigners have sold more than $1.2 billion worth of Indian shares so far this month, after buying $4.3 billion in October, trimming their net buying to about $16 billion so far in 2007.   \par \par \uc2 Intermittent dollar purchases by oil companies put further pressure on the local unit, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 While capital flows into the economy continued, the volumes were not as expected by many participants, who bought the rupee early in the day expecting strong flows on strong Asian cues.   \par \par \uc2 When the market sensed that the rupee was not going to be bolstered by overseas flows, many dealers reversed their bets, helping drive the rupee from its intraday peak of 39.58.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index         <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)              <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by C.J. Kurrien; Editing by Ranjit Gangadharan)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|SNI|RBN|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071129e3bq000co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
