\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee gains on Asia stocks; cbank watched   \b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2007\par 04:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 26 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee strengthened on Monday as share market gains in Mumbai and elsewhere in Asia raised expectations that foreigners would resume buying local shares, though concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighed.   \par \par \uc2 At 9:45 a.m. (0415 GMT), the rupee <INR=IN> was at 39.605/610 per dollar, moving up from Friday's finish of 39.71/72, its weakest since Oct. 23. It 39.16 earlier this month, its highest since March 1998.   \par \par \uc2 "We're likely to trade in a range today, but it all depends on where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to take the pair," said a senior dealer with a foreign bank who expected the rupee to trade in a 39.55-39.65 range on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The Indian stock market <.BSESN> was up 2 percent at 0450 GMT, and Asian stocks were also up after investors were encouraged by a robust start to the U.S. holiday shopping period. See [ID:nHKG294081]   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the buoyancy in stocks lifted sentiment among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors, but fears of a U.S. slowdown persisted.   \par \par \uc2 Foreigners have sold more than $1 billion worth of Indian shares so far this month, after buying $4.3 billion in October, trimming their net buying to $16.1 billion so far in 2007.   \par \par \uc2 The market also expects some foreign funds to repatriate profits before the end of the year.   \par \par \uc2 Adding to the caution, dealers are wary about making big bets on a stronger rupee because of the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought nearly $52 billion in the first nine months of 2007 in a bid to temper the rupee's ascent, and traders said it has been active in October and November.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------\par                     MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index         <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)              <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by C.J. Kurrien; Editing by )   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071129e3bq0005p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
