\par\par \b \uc2 FOCUS: Indian Bonds In Doldrums As Govt Soaks Up Liquidity   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vipin V. Nair   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,118 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2007\par 10:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bonds will likely remain stuck in the doldrums for the next few months, as official steps to drain excess liquidity from the financial system have left local banks with less cash to chase prices higher.   \par \par \uc2 Concerns that rising oil prices may lift wholesale price inflation from around five-year lows now could also take wind out of the sails of the bond market, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 "There is little potential for a sizable rally," said Vikas Agarwal, fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase Bank. "Tight liquidity will most likely restrain the market's performance. It is likely to remain lackluster."   \par \par \uc2 Bond prices have been trading in a narrow range in thin volumes for the past several weeks, as investors wait for clearer signals on the outlook for liquidity and inflation. The steady trading follows a mild rally last month, when prices got a boost from banks putting extra cash into the bond market.   \par \par \uc2 At 0915GMT Monday, the benchmark 10-year government bond yield was at 7.88%.   \par \par \uc2 Many analysts expect the benchmark yield to stay between 7.75%-7.95% until March 31, when the current fiscal year ends.   \par \par \uc2 Banks' surplus cash position is a crucial factor for the direction of the Indian government bond market as banks hold over 60% of the INR12.3 trillion in outstanding government bonds. Rules require lenders to keep 25% of their deposits in government bonds, gold or cash as reserves known as the statutory liquidity ratio.   \par \par \uc2 For months, liquidity had grown rapidly. Much of it was a byproduct of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was fighting a rise in the rupee. The RBI's coffers swelled by $52 billion in the nine months through September, with the bulk of the rupee it sold ending up with local banks.   \par \par \uc2 But the tide has changed. The RBI has been increasing efforts to soak up some of that liquidity out of concern it could fuel inflationary pressures.   \par \par \uc2 Excess cash in the system fell drastically after the RBI drained INR160 billion by raising the cash reserve ratio - the share of deposits banks must keep with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cash - by 50 basis points to 7.50% from Nov. 10. Regular debt sales by the government and sterilization bond issues by the RBI have also absorbed sizable amounts of banks' surplus cash.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the RBI could ratchet up the reserve requirement even further in coming months, given its stated intention to keep liquidity in a check.   \par \par \uc2 "Right now, the RBI's attempt is to pre-empt excess liquidity and moderate inflationary expectations," said Mahendra Jajoo, Senior Vice President at  \uc2 ABN Amro Asset Management\uc2  (India) Ltd.   \par \par \uc2 Jajoo said the RBI may hike the reserve ratio again before the end of March.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign Fund Inflows Ease   \par \par \uc2 A reduction in foreign money flowing into India may also lead to less liquidity entering the banking system.   \par \par \uc2 India's stock market recently has wavered along with other markets on fears of a global rout driven by the U.S. subprime mortgage crisis, with some foreign investors starting to pull some money out.   \par \par \uc2 The latest data from Securities and Exchange Board of India show foreign investors have sold a net of $1.10 billion worth Indian stocks so far in November, compared with net purchases of $5.07 billion in October, when the benchmark Sensex crossed 20,000 points for the first time.   \par \par \uc2 The Sensex was at 19,282.07 points Monday afternoon.   \par \par \uc2 Recent curbs on foreign fund inflows into local stocks, such as a ban on the issue of participatory notes with derivatives as underlying assets, are also expected to slow the flow of overseas money into India.   \par \par \uc2 Such developments will likely ease upward pressure on the rupee, thereby minimizing the need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. The result: less rupee hitting into the system.   \par \par \uc2 "The stock market is now consolidating and upward pressure on the rupee is not much there," said Ashish Nigam, head of fixed income at DBS Cholamandalam AMC Limited.   \par \par \uc2 What's more, advance tax payments by companies by mid-December will also strain banks' cash position again, Nigam said. Indian companies are expected to withdraw INR350 billion to INR400 billion from banks to pay their quarterly advance taxes, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Hawkish RBI Would Weigh On Bonds   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the bond market could still fare all right.   \par \par \uc2 They point to demand from insurance companies and employees' provident funds for long-tenure bonds as one factor that could breathe some life into the otherwise dull bond market.   \par \par \uc2 Indian rules require life insurers to invest 25% of their assets in government bonds while provident funds must keep 40% of their assets in such securities.   \par \par \uc2 Improvement in finances could mean the federal government may borrow lower than its projected INR590 billion from the market during the second half of the current fiscal year, which will likely reduce the supply of bonds.   \par \par \uc2 "I won't be surprised if the government doesn't borrow the full amount," said ABN Amro's Jajoo.   \par \par \uc2 Still, most analysts say the balance of forces points to a steady bond market for now.   \par \par \uc2 Indian banks don't need to boost their holdings of bonds in their reserves to meet statutory liquidity ratio requirements.  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's Agarwal reckons banks already hold around 30% of their reserves in bonds compared with the mandated 25%.   \par \par \uc2 "The SLR position is still comfortable and there is little urgency on the part of banks to buy bonds to meet their requirements. So that factor is more or less relegated to the background now," says Agarwal.   \par \par \uc2 Also, while inflation has eased to its lowest level in five years at just 3.01% in the week ended Nov. 10, lingering concerns the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take steps to keep inflationary pressures at bay could also keep bond prices in check.   \par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to hold rates steady at least until end of March after a string of rate hikes that began three years ago quelled price pressures.   \par \par \uc2 But if oil makes a run at $100 a barrel and the government decides to raise local fuel prices in order to pass some of the higher cost along to consumers, the RBI may stick to a tightening bias.   \par \par \uc2 "One thing is very clear: times aren't going to be easy" for bonds, said Ritesh Jain, head of fixed income at Kotak Mahindra Asset Management Company.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Vipin V. Nair; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; vipin.nair@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 26-11-07 0931GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1174 4025 5008   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071126e3bq000fw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
