\par\par \b \uc2 Rupee slips on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , oil, capital outflows   \b0\par\par\uc2 By C.J. Kurrien   \par \uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2007\par 13:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 23 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee slumped to its lowest in a month in late trade on Friday, weighed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollar purchases by an oil company and a rising risk aversion among foreign investors, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 39.71/72 per dollar, its weakest since Oct. 23, and slipping sharply from from an early high of 39.47. It ended at 39.53/54 on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 "There are quite a few factors hurting the rupee at the moment, and things would have been worse if the dollar was not so weak," said the chief dealer with a private bank.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said overseas investors were cutting positions in Indian assets and repatriating end-year profits, and an outflow of funds from a oversubscribed public offering had also contributed to the rupee's weakness.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said offshore demand for dollars against the rupee, an indication that some foreign funds were paring holdings in relatively risky assets, had weighed on sentiment.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in spurts through the day, selling the rupee to ensure it closed weaker than 30.70, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought nearly $52 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first nine months of the year in a bid to temper the rupee's ascent.   \par \par \uc2 Still, losses were limited as the dollar remained near record lows agains the euro and a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on renewed fears about the fallout of the U.S. housing crisis, and a growing view that the Federal Reserve would cut interest rates again next month.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                     MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index         <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)              <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by John Mair)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071126e3bn000ou\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
