\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  cbank says actively  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  mkt   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Surojit Gupta and Rajkumar Ray   \par \uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2007\par 07:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more quotes, analysts' views)   \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Nov 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and will use all instruments to maintain price stability and manage liquidity, its deputy governor said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 "We have a managed float, but we do \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market actively," Rakesh Mohan told a business gathering.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of India bought almost $52 billion in the first nine months of 2007 to curb rupee gains against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The local unit has risen about 12 percent this year but eased on Thursday to close at 39.53/54 per dollar, slipping from the previous day's 39.390/395.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, it hit 39.16, its strongest in nearly 10 years, but was pushed lower by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar purchases.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said capital inflows into Asia's third-largest economy would continue to be robust in coming months, and this would keep the rupee <INR=IN> under pressure.   \par \par \uc2 "In view of large capital inflows, we had to use the instrument of cash reserve ratio (CRR)," Mohan later told reporters. "We are ready to use all instruments -- market stabilisation scheme, liquidity adjustment facility, and CRR -- depending on the situation."   \par \par \uc2 Officials are finding it difficult to manage large inflows with foreign funds pumping about $17 billion into local stocks.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly raised the cash reserve ratio and sold market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds (MSS) to soak up surplus cash with banks, and ease price pressures.   \par \par \uc2 "Inflows depend on sentiments and the fundamentals of the economy. I don't see flows moderating in the coming months," said D.K. Joshi, economist with domestic rating agency Crisil.   \par \par \uc2 "It may not be at this pace, but it will continue to pressurise the rupee."   \par \par \uc2 Mohan listed surging oil prices and food price inflation globally as risks to domestic inflation but said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance remained unchanged from its October review.   \par \par \uc2 "Our objective are to ensure price stability, maintenance of growth and financial stability," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Mohan said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been successful in moderating prices and loan growth in sectors like real estate, housing and capital markets by raising the risk weightage on bank loans to these sectors. (Additional reporting Saikat Chatterjee in Mumbai, editing by Mike Peacock)   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNI|ABX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071122e3bm000g1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
