\par\par \b \uc2 India has no plans to further curb inflows-minister   \b0\par\par\uc2 296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2007\par 09:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Nov 20 (Reuters) - India has no plans for further checks on capital inflows that have been a major support for a strengthening rupee in recent months, a minister said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have pumped around $17 billion so far this year into the Indian stock market, sending the main index soaring to a series of life highs and pushing the rupee <INR=IN> up to near-decade peaks.   \par \par \uc2 "There is no proposal for more checks on inflow of capital at present," Junior Finance Minister Pawan Kumar Bansal said in a written reply to Parliament.   \par \par \uc2 Last month, the Securities and Exchange Board of India, tightened rules on foreign investors' use of an investment instrument known as a participatory note, in a step authorities said aimed both to improve flow transparency and moderate the amount of funds coming into the country.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruled that money raised overseas by local firms in excess of $20 million must be spent abroad.   \par \par \uc2 India's economy has grown by an average of 8.6 percent over the last four years and the government wants to lift annual expansion to double digits.   \par \par \uc2 But officials are finding it difficult to manage large capital inflows while keeping inflation in check.   \par \par \uc2 "To the extent that Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a view to reduce undue volatility, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, such appreciation was moderated," Bansal said.   \par \par \uc2 "A depreciation of the U.S. dollar against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and increased capital flows have led to appreciation of the rupee vis-a-vis the U.S. dollar," he said.   \par \par \uc2 ($1=39.3 rupees) (Reporting by Rajkumar Ray)   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/INFLOWS|LANGEN|SNI|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071120e3bk000og\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
