\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-India rupee to gain in '08 but rise seen slower   \b0\par\par\uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2007\par 04:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * What: India's rupee seen rising in 2008 but at slower pace   \par \par \uc2 * Forecast: 38.88/dollar by end March, 37.63 by Dec-end 2008   \par \par \uc2 * Risks: Rupee seen less affected by the U.S. slowdown than other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By C.J. Kurrien   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 15 (Reuters) - Capital inflows should drive the Indian rupee to more gains against the dollar next year, but its climb will be less steep than in 2007 if foreign investment slows in tandem with the U.S. economy, a Reuters poll shows.   \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> hit a near-decade high of 39.16 per dollar this month, driven by foreign funds which have pumped a record $17 billion into India's booming stock market this year, helping the rupee gain about 12 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at 39.30 per dollar on Wednesday and a Reuters poll of 22 market participants this week forecasts it will stand at 38.88 by the end of March 2008. Analysts then see it gaining further 3.3 percent to 37.63 by the end of 2008.   \par \par \uc2 "Inflows will dictate that the rupee continues to appreciate at a fairly modest rate," said  \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Asia economist Robert Prior-Wandesforde.   \par \par \uc2 Rapid economic growth averaging at 8.6 percent in the past four years has lured foreign capital into India's markets but the rupee's sharp rise has upset the country's efforts to boost exports this year.   \par \par \uc2 Indian firms, particularly in textiles and handicrafts, have complained it is hurting business and Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram meets textiles sector representatives later on Thursday to discuss the impact of the rupee's strength.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said a slowdown in the U.S. economy was likely to slow overseas investment flows into Asia's third-largest economy, though the rupee will be less affected by a U.S. downturn than many other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They say India's economy is less dependent on exports to the United States than many of its peers.   \par \par \uc2 "(India is) clearly among the least exposed to the U.S. and one would expect it to survive pretty well any downturn in the U.S. spending," said Prior-Wandesforde. Still, any easing of capital inflows would give the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively against the rupee, as would any reduction in global crude oil prices.   \par \par \uc2 "If there is a U.S. slowdown ... commodity price concerns will moderate and the RBI will be a little more comfortable and perhaps a little more aggressive in aiding rupee depreciation," said Abheek Barua, chief economist with  \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2  .   \par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a record $11.87 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September to temper the rupee's rise after a U.S. rate cut drew huge inflows into the stock market. It bought $51.8 billion in the first nine months of 2007.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par    FORECASTS FOR THE INDIAN RUPEE PER U.S. DOLLAR\par                      March 2008     June 2008    Dec 2008\par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Axis Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2                  38.00           38.00       37.00\par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2            38.00           39.00       37.00\par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Baroda\f28 \uc2 \f2             38.50           37.70       36.60\par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Nova Scotia\f28 \uc2 \f