\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee climbs on flow outlook, cbank watched   \b0\par\par\uc2 343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2007\par 03:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Updates to open)   \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, Nov 14 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose on Wednesday as dealers bet on overseas investment flows into local equities on the back of firmer Asian markets, but dollar purchases by oil refiners and likely \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could limit gains.   \par \par \uc2  At 9:30 a.m. (0400 GMT), the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was at 39.342/352 per dollar, rising smartly from Tuesday's finish of 39.42/43, and heading back towards a near-decade high of 39.16 hit last week.   \par \par \uc2  "Foreign banks are buying dollars -- most likely they're expecting some good inflows today, and for the stock index to rise," said the chief dealer with a state-run bank, who expects the rupee to trade in a 39.40-39.33 range on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  Asian stocks rose as investors, encouraged by a rally on Wall Street, snapped up shares that had been hit hard by four straight sessions of losses. See [ID:nHKG143937].   \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks were partly helped by comments from the chief executive of the  \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group\uc2   that the investment bank would not face big credit losses stemming from the U.S. subprime market crisis.   \par \par \uc2  The yen fell, indicating a renewal in risk appetite, and a resumption of carry trades, where investors sell low yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in favour of high yielding assets, like the rupee.   \par \par \uc2  Still, the  prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighed on the rupee, with resistance expected at around 39.30.   \par \par \uc2  Data released on Tuesday showed the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a record $11.87 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September to slow the rupee's rise.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has played an active role in tempering the rupee's ascent in October and November as well.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index         <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)              <INR=IN>\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by C.J. Kurrien; Editing by Ranjit Gangadharan)\par \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|SNI|RBN|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071117e3be00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
