\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 3- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  raises  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  bond ceiling   \b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2007\par 14:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's firepower to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday by increasing the ceiling on the amount of special bonds it can issue to absorb the rupees generated by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The limit on the bonds, known as market stabilisation bonds, has been lifted to 2.5 trillion rupees ($63.6 billion) from 2.0 trillion, said a statement from the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) released after markets had closed.   \par \par \uc2 Following are some quick comments from analysts.   \par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:   \par \par \uc2 LATEST:   \par \par \uc2 RUPA REGE NITSURE, CHIEF ECONOMIST AT BANK OF BARODA, MUMBAI:   \par \par \uc2 "This clearly shows that the problem of inflows is much more serious than what policymakers had anticipated and the Reserve Bank of India is left with no alternative but to use all instruments in its arsenal.   \par \par \uc2 "The recent cash reserve ratio hike and this increase in market stabilisation bond ceiling will ensure that the upward bias in the interest rate structure will continue."   \par \par \uc2 EARLIER:   \par \par \uc2 D.K. JOSHI, PRINCIPAL ECONOMIST, CRISIL, MUMBAI:   \par \par \uc2 "There are two options before Reserve Bank of India -- either raise the cash reserve ratio or the MSS limit -- to manage excess liquidity. They are using both.   \par \par \uc2 "With the kind of capital inflows witnessed in last few months, they have to suck the excess liquidity. The situation warranted so, as government seems unlikely to to put further curbs on capital inflows.   \par \par \uc2 "Without this (increase in MSS limit), there is a danger of an upward pressure on inflation."   \par \par \uc2 ARUN KAUL, GENERAL MANAGER (TREASURY), PUNJAB NATIONAL BANK, NEW DELHI:   \par \par \uc2 "We were expecting some action on this front. We are still seeing lot of inflows. This (increase in MSS limit) will certainly help suck out excess liquidity and stem inflationary expectations, especially at a time when oil prices are surging.   \par \par \uc2 "There will be some upward pressure on yields of bonds."   \par \par \uc2 SAUMITRA CHAUDHURI, ECONOMIC ADVISER, DOMESTIC RATING AGENCY, ICRA, NEW DELHI:   \par \par \uc2 "This is an appropriate step. There has been ill-informed speculation in the past that the MSS bond limit will constrain Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." (Reporting by Anurag Joshi, Surojit Gupta and Rajkumar Ray, Editing by Mark Williams)   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-CBANK/MSS   (INSTANT VIEW)|LANGEN|SNI|ABN|M|E|RBN|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071110e3b7000aa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
