\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  raises cbank firepower for rupee  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anurag Joshi   \par \uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2007\par 15:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, analyst quotes)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday after the rupee touched a near-decade high, and analysts said the move would help ease the pressure of huge capital inflows.   \par \par \uc2 A statement from the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) after market hours said the ceiling on bonds it uses to suck cash from the banking system had been lifted by a quarter to 2.5 trillion rupees ($63.6 billion) for the fiscal year 2007/08.   \par \par \uc2 Strong inflows into the world's fastest-growing major economy after China have pushed up the rupee, which is now close to a 9-\u189\'00\'BD year high at 39.3 rupees to a dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI has bought close to $40 billion over the first eight months this year.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling market stabilisation scheme (MSS) bonds since March to soak up cash surpluses with banks, which enters the system when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars to check the rupee's rise.   \par \par \uc2 "There are two options before Reserve Bank of India -- either raise the cash reserve ratio or the MSS limit -- to manage excess liquidity. They are using both," said D.K. Joshi, principal economist, at Crisil in Mumbai.   \par \par \uc2 "With the kind of capital inflows witnessed in last few months, they have to suck the excess liquidity. The situation warranted so, as government seems unlikely to put further curbs on capital inflows.   \par \par \uc2 "Without this (increase in MSS limit), there is a danger of an upward pressure on inflation."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement added the threshold at which the new limit would be reviewed would now be 2.35 trillion rupees.   \par \par \uc2 "With the MSS auction of treasury bills held today, the MSS outstanding (face value) will be 1.8 trillion rupees as on Nov. 8," the statement added.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts had expected the move.   \par \par \uc2 "Assuming capital flows revert to normal patterns, we would still expect $3-4 billion monthly net surplus in the coming months, and by extension dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of similar amounts," DBS said in a research note on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 In a policy review last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would raise banks' cash reserve ratio by 50 basis points to 7.50 percent. The move, which takes effect on Nov. 10, will drain 160 billion rupees from the banking system.   \par \par \uc2 After the policy, RBI Governor Yaga Venugopal Reddy told reporters that a liquidity overhang in the system is the only area of concern for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "All the indicators point to a greater normalcy than before and the only area of concern is the overhang of liquidity," he said. (Additional reporting by Rajkumar Ray and Surojit Gupta) (Editing by Mark Williams)   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-MSS/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|SNI|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071107e3b7001kl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
