\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Bonds Down On Liquidity Tightening Fears; Rupee Flat   \b0\par\par\uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2007\par 13:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest     Change \par  USD/INR           39.31      Unchanged \par  7.99% 2017 Bond   7.86%      +1BP \par  Call Rate         6.10%      Unchanged \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  October           0.45       +0.01 \par  *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--India government bond prices fell Monday on concerns banks' excess cash may soon fall, damping demand for securities, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Bond prices will likely move in a narrow range in the coming sessions, they said.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 7.99% 2017 bond ended at INR100.80, down from INR100.87 at Friday's close.   \par \par \uc2 "The bond prices will stay rangebound in the coming days. And if at all there is a price action, it will be on the negative side, because I expect the liquidity to tighten," said Mohan Shenoi, treasurer at  \uc2 Kotak Mahindra Bank.\par \par \uc2 Excess cash in the banking system will likely fall in the coming days as a 50 basis point increase in the cash reserve ratio of banks from Saturday will absorb about INR160 billion.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of India raised the CRR at its monetary policy review last week to contain the surplus cash in the banking system which has the potential to fuel inflation.   \par \par \uc2 The CRR, which is the share of deposits banks must keep in cash with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will rise to 7.50% from Saturday after the RBI hiked the ratio for the fourth time since January.   \par \par \uc2 A sovereign debt auction worth INR80 billion scheduled for Thursday will also soak up banks' cash.   \par \par \uc2 Banks are the largest buyers in the Indian debt market, and any fall in their surplus cash affects demand for bonds.   \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Indian rupee ended unchanged against the dollar Monday after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from nine-and-a-half-year lows.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR39.31, unchanged from Friday's close.   \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar moved in a tight range for most of the session, heavy selling by some corporates pulled it down to INR39.22 in the last hour of trading.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the dollar bounced back to its familiar territory of INR39.31/INR39.32, where it has found support in recent sessions from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the RBI will let this level be broken convincingly in the coming sessions. The dollar/rupee will continue to stay in a range of INR39.30-INR39.40 band," a dealer at a private bank said.   \par \par \uc2 However, India's finance minister, P. Chidambaram, said the rupee is set to gain further, in line with the growth in the economy.   \par \par \uc2 "If the economy is growing at an average rate of 8.6%, the rupee is bound to strengthen and therefore we must learn to adjust to trade in the environment where rupee will strengthen," Chidambaram told a gathering of industrialists in Mumbai.   \par \par \uc2 -By Vipin V. Nair, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; vipin.nair@dowjones.com [ 05-11-07 1243GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071105e3b5000r6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
