\par\par \b \uc2 India Bonds Up On Hopes Of More Foreign Invest; Rupee Gains   \b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2007\par 14:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest     Change \par  USD/INR           39.32      -0.06 \par  7.99% 2017 Bond   7.84%      -2 BPs \par  Call Rate         6.10%      +5 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  September         0.51       Unchanged \par  *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bond prices rose Wednesday after the capital market regulator said the current ceiling for foreign investment in local bonds may be raised, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 7.99% 2017-dated bond ended at INR100.98, up from INR100.85 at Tuesday's close.   \par \par \uc2 "The proposal to raise the foreign investment limit in bonds will increase market participation," said a state-owned bank's bond dealer.   \par \par \uc2 Under current regulations, foreign investors can invest up to $3.2 billion in government bonds and up to $1.5 billion in corporate bonds per year.   \par \par \uc2 "There is an appetite for bonds. There are limits on foreign participation in bond markets at this point in time, but you will see that moving away," Securities and Exchange Board of India Chairman M. Damodaran said at an event in New Delhi.   \par \par \uc2 Lower-than-expected yield cutoffs at a treasury bill auction by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped bond prices to edge higher.   \par \par \uc2 "The market was expecting the yield on the 91-day t-bill to come in around 7.33%-7.34% while the yield cutoff at the auction was 7.31% and that has pushed the bonds up a bit," said the state-run bank dealer.   \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the rupee inched closer to its nine-and-a-half-year high of INR39.27 against the dollar hit earlier this month, helped by robust foreign fund inflows into local stocks.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR39.32, compared with INR39.38 at Tuesday's close.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the dollar at INR39.30.   \par \par \uc2 "The dollar-rupee's movement tomorrow (Thursday) completely depends on what the Fed will do," said a private bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve is widely expected to cut its benchmark rate by 25 basis points later in the global day.   \par \par \uc2 Such a move is likely to attract more foreign capital into countries like India, already battling with torrential foreign fund inflows.   \par \par \uc2 The dealer said the market had already factored in a 25 basis-point rate cut by the Federal Reserve, but a 50 basis-point cut would push the rupee closer to INR39 against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "If that happens the rupee will jump tomorrow," he said.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Vipin V. Nair; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; vipin.nair@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 31-10-07 1318GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071031e3av0018k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
