\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Hits 9 1/2 Year High; Bonds Gain   \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2007\par 14:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest     Change \par  USD/INR           39.31      -0.11 \par  7.99% 2017 Bond   7.89%      -3 BPs \par  Call Rate         6.20%      +15 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  September         0.47       +0.08 \par *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee rose Wednesday to its highest level against the dollar since March 1998, boosted by foreign fund inflows into local stocks.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The rupee will likely extend its upward momentum in the coming sessions as foreign investors continue to pump more money into India's stocks markets, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR39.31 against INR39.42 on Tuesday's finish.   \par \par \uc2 R. K. Gurumoorthy, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at  \uc2 ING Vysya Bank\uc2  said the dollar is likely to fall to INR39.20 by Monday.   \par \par \uc2 "As long as the stock market-(related foreign fund) flows remain buoyant, the dollar will continue to fall by around INR0.05 every day," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Strong foreign fund inflows into the capital market have been a key factor that has helped the rupee gain by over 11% against the dollar since January.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have brought about $15 billion in to Indian equity markets in 2007, well over the $7.99 billion they invested in 2006.   \par \par \uc2 Indian stock markets rose for the second consecutive session Wednesday. The Bombay Stock Exchange's 30-stock index, the Sensex, was up 378.01 points, or 2.1%, to end at 18,658.25.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily during the session to check any sharp gains in the rupee.   \par \par \uc2 In the debt market, government bond prices rose as banks and mutual funds, flush with funds, bought sovereign papers.   \par \par \uc2 Lower-than-expected yield cutoffs at a treasury bill auction, which indicated strong demand, also boosted investor sentiment.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 7.99% 2017 bond ended at INR100.60, up from INR100.44 on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said bond prices will move in a narrow range in the next session as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is slated to sell sovereign debt of INR180 billion on Thursday and Friday.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Vipin V. Nair; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; vipin.nair@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-10-07 1234GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071010e3aa000p0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
