\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Gains As Local Stocks Hit Record High; Bonds Up   \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2007\par 15:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    \par  As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest     Change \par  USD/INR           39.42      -0.02 \par  7.99% 2017 Bond   7.92%      -1 BP \par  Call Rate         6.05%      +5 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  September         0.39       Unchanged \par *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee gained against the U.S. dollar Tuesday as global investors poured more money into the record-breaking local stock market, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The rupee will likely strengthen further and may even test its more than nine-and-a-half-year high of 39.36 to a dollar if the stock market sustains its momentum, they said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR39.42, down from INR39.44 at Monday's close.   \par \par \uc2 "It looks like the FIIs (foreign institutional investors) pumped in a lot of money today after the surprise rally of the Sensex," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with a private bank.   \par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's 30-stock index, the Sensex, gained 788.85 points, or 4.5%, to end at 18280.24, a record close.   \par \par \uc2 Strong foreign fund inflows into the capital market have been a key factor that has boosted the rupee nearly 11% against the dollar since January.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have pumped $3.53 billion into Indian stocks from Sept. 26 to Oct. 5. In the year to Oct.5, they invested $14.54 billion in Indian equity markets, well over the $7.99 billion they brought in during 2006.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to stem the rupee's sharp gains.   \par \par \uc2 "But the RBI doesn't seem to be protecting any particular level. They are only trying to reduce the volatility in the market," the dealer said.   \par \par \uc2 In the debt market, government bond prices gained marginally, but trading volumes were thin as players awaited INR180 billion of sovereign bond sales scheduled for later this week.   \par \par \uc2 The 7.99% 2017 bond ended at INR100.44, up from INR100.37 Monday.   \par \par \uc2 "The market will be rangebound in the coming sessions since there is no clarity on what steps the RBI will take to contain the excess liquidity in the system," said a private bank dealer.   \par \par \uc2 Many traders are speculating that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may raise banks' cash reserve ratio again to absorb excess funds from the system.   \par \par \uc2 The CRR, which is the share of deposits banks must hold with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cash, is currently at 7%, after the RBI raised it three times this year.   \par \par \uc2 Banks are the largest buyers of bonds in India, and any dip in their surplus cash usually affects demand for debt.   \par \par \uc2 -By Vipin V. Nair, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; vipin.nair@dowjones.com [ 09-10-07 1311GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071009e3a90014o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
