\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee eases from early peak, stocks weigh   \b0\par\par\uc2 363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2007\par 05:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to early trade)   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Oct 4 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose to its strongest level in 9-\u189\'00\'BD years on Thursday, but then eased as a weak opening in the stock market raised some worries that the recent rapid pace of foreign inflows could slow.   \par \par \uc2 At 10:10 a.m. (0440 GMT), the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was 39.60/61 per dollar, having risen as far as 39.5450, its strongest level since April 1998. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had closed at 39.575/585 on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 "It's a bit of a treacherous level at the moment, because if stocks slip we could see the rupee move down sharply -- the market is treading very carefully," said a dealer with a private bank.   \par \par \uc2 India's benchmark share index <.BSESN> opened weaker on Thursday. It has risen for 11 sessions in a row, its longest run of gains in four years, and increased by about 15 percent in that time. It is up more than 25 percent since Aug. 21, when it ended at a three-month closing low.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign buying of stocks has been a key driver of those gains and have also boosted the rupee. Data shows foreign funds bought about $4.2 billion of stocks in the nine sessions after the U.S. Federal Reserve cut interest rates on Sept. 18.   \par \par \uc2 Further, the prospect of aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighed on the rupee, dealers said. Traders say the Reserve Bank of India has been buying dollars to cap the rupee, which has gained nearly 12 percent this year.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $38.1 billion in the first seven months of 2007 to slow the rupee's rise, the most recent data shows.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will review a limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds if asked by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram said on Wednesday, and analysts said the ceiling would have to rise at least by a third to 2 trillion rupees ($50.5 billion) if the rupee's rise was to be checked.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|RBN|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071007e3a400043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
