\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  may review limit on market  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  bonds   \b0\par\par\uc2 175 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2007\par 10:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The Indian government will review a limit on market stabilisation scheme (MSS) bonds if there is a request from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 "If there is a request from the Reserve Bank of India, we will certainly review it," Chidambaram told reporters when asked whether the government was contemplating raising the MSS ceiling.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI has stepped up selling MSS bonds as efforts to cap the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains following bold rate cuts by the U.S. Federal Reserve released rupees <INR=IN> into the system.   \par \par \uc2 Outstanding MSS issues stood at 1.176 trillion rupees ($29.5 billion rupees) as of Sept. 21, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed. It sold 100 billion rupees of these bonds last week.   \par \par \uc2 The current MSS limit is 1.5 trillion rupees and the cap has to be raised if outstanding issues exceed 1.35 trillion rupees.   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/BONDS  (URGENT)|LANGEN|SNI|ABN|M|E|RBN|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071006e3a30008i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
