\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  Rupee Flat;RBI  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Negates Stk Gains; Bonds Dn   \b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2007\par 14:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest     Change \par  USD/INR           39.72      +0.01 \par  7.99% 2017 Bond   7.90%      +1 BP \par  Call Rate         7.20%      +120BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  September         0.49       -0.03 \par *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee ended flat Thursday as importer demand and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support for the dollar negated gains from surging local stocks.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR39.72, off its intraday high of INR39.90. It had closed at INR39.71 Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee had a very volatile trading day, strengthening in early trade to regain its nine-year peak of INR39.62 to the dollar, but weakening as the RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "The Indian rupee's move has been too fast and severe. I expect some fall from current levels," said R.V.S. Sridhar, vice president (treasury) at  \uc2 Axis Bank.\par \par \uc2 The RBI has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions to buy the dollar in a bid to arrest the rise of the rupee, which has strengthened more than 11% in 2007, making it Asia's best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "The RBI was probably again in the market today, but I don't see the logic behind it buying at one point and vanishing the next," said the chief dealer at a state-run bank.   \par \par \uc2 Local stocks surged for an eighth consecutive session, further bolstering hopes of more foreign fund inflows.   \par \par \uc2 Indian shares rose 1.4% to end at a record high of 17150.56.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have poured nearly $1.9 billion into Indian equities since the U.S. Federal Reserve cut its benchmark rate.   \par \par \uc2 Sridhar expects the dollar to rise to INR40 in the near term.   \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, government bond prices fell as banks and primary dealers preferred selling bonds to book profits as the quarter comes to a close.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 7.99% 2017 bond closed at INR100.55, as against INR100.64 at the previous close.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the market to take its cue from inflation data, due 0630 GMT Friday.   \par \par \uc2 India's inflation rate likely rose to 3.41% on the year in the week ended Sept. 15, up from 3.32% the previous week, a Dow Jones Newswires poll of five market participants found.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI expects inflation of around 5% for the current financial year.   \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said in a note it expects a supply overhang to pressure bonds in the immediate term, but retains a "constructive outlook" in the medium term.   \par \par \uc2 -By Subhadip Sircar, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; djn.in@dowjones.com [ 27-09-07 1228GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070927e39r000um\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
