\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSTS' VIEW-Indian rupee strengthens past 40/dlr to 9-yr high   \b0\par\par\uc2 888 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2007\par 09:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, Sept 20 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee <INR=IN> strengthened past 40 per dollar on Thursday for the first time in more than nine years, after a cut in U.S. rates earlier in the week brightened the outlook for high-yielding assets.   \par \par \uc2  The partially convertible rupee rose to as much as 39.88 -- last traded in May 1998 -- on Thursday, taking its gains against the dollar this year to 11 percent, the most of any Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily this year to limit the rupee's gains -- the most recent data shows it bought more than $38 billion in the first seven months of 2007.   \par \par \uc2  Following are comments from economists and market analysts on what the rupee's rally means for policy makers and where it is headed in the near term.   \par \par \uc2  ***********************************************************   \par \par \uc2  GAURAV KAPUR, SENIOR ECONOMIST,  \uc2 ABN AMRO BANK\uc2 , MUMBAI:   \par \par \uc2  "The rupee would continue to witness sharp appreciation pressures in the near term, given the exuberance in the equities market, wider interest rate differentials and the general weakness of the U.S. dollar."   \par \par \uc2  "However, high crude oil prices and Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will counter these pressures and the rupee could see some weakness towards the end of this month."   \par \par \uc2  "The oil price spiral above $80 per barrel has been ignored by the market till now, and that remains the key risk to a sustained appreciation of the Indian unit."   \par \par \uc2  NIZAM IDRIS, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST,  \uc2 UBS\uc2 , SINGAPORE:   \par \par \uc2  "It hasn't broken the 40 level convincingly. The RBI could just push it back up over 40 and defend that for a bit longer.   \par \par \uc2  "But that would require significant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would mean pressure on money supply to accelerate. Currently we do not have significant issues with inflation. Possible inflation would remain benign for the rest of year given the base effect. But food prices are higher, oil prices also increasing. Those are the two factors which could see RBI choosing a significantly lower line in the sand - possibly even near 38 for example."   \par \par \uc2  "For now that inflation pressure is still not there and it hasn't actually broken 40 convincingly."   \par \par \uc2  D.K. JOSHI, PRINCIPAL ECONOMIST, CRISIL, MUMBAI:   \par \par \uc2  "It will be around 40 level even after three months. Right now, the sudden surge is euphoria driven. There are huge capital inflows and the expectation is for the rupee to appreciate, Fed has eased. All this has caused the sudden appreciation of the rupee, and I think it will stabilise once the euphoria stabilises."   \par \par \uc2  "I think the RBI will try to protect it as much as they can because their ability to protect is also limited. I would say if the scenario continues, we will see another cash reserve ratio (CRR) hike so the RBI can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  "The Market Stabilisation Scheme ceiling (MSS) has also been increased. It comes with a fiscal cost to the government. It is very difficult to say if they will increase the quota."   \par \par \uc2  HAN-SIA YEO, ANALYST AT BANK OF AMERICA, SINGAPORE:   \par \par \uc2  "There's still headroom for MSS. Inflation is still low now plus credit growth has moderated. So the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it wants to. The RBI also hasn't been very eager to withdraw liquidity, though the liquidity cushion could be in part due to global financial market volatility."   \par \par \uc2  "But I don't see this leading up to a likely cash reserve requirement increase in the near term, as broader economic conditions are very different from earlier and so the same aggressive policy response may not be that forthcoming."   \par \par \uc2  "We expect the rupee to end 2007 at 39.50 per dollar."   \par \par \uc2  SONAL VARMA, ECONOMIST, LEHMAN BROTHER, MUMBAI   \par \par \uc2  "A resurgence in capital inflows will complicate the RBI's monetary policy. We expect the RBI to allow some rupee appreciation, encourage capital outflows and use the MSS and the CRR hikes to sterilise inflows. Strong capital inflows are also likely, at the margin, to make the RBI less willing to hike rates, lest it could provoke even stronger capital inflows.   \par \par \uc2  Our research shows that of the Asian countries, India is among the least exposed to a global economic slowdown, and the interest rate differential is now strongly in favour to attract strong capital inflows. This will likely put pressure on the rupee to appreciate. We expect rupee/dollar at 40.0 by end 2007 and 37.5 by end 2008."   \par \par \uc2  PETER REDWARD, HEAD OF RATES RESEARCH, EMERGING ASIA, BARCLAYS CAPITAL, SINGAPORE:   \par \par \uc2  " \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  forecasts the Indian rupee to strengthen to 39.75 by year-end, but we see upside risk to our forecast. We project the current account deficit of $17.3 billion in FY08, to be largely financed by net FDI inflows and concessional loans."   \par \par \uc2  "In addition, India is likely to experience further portfolio capital inflows and as U.S. interest rates decline, growth in non-resident Indian repatriation flows."   \par \par \uc2  "In our opinion, liquidity is likely to rapidly return to the system as the RBI acts to stem rupee appreciation and injects liquidity into the money market. Consequently, we will be closely watching both the overnight rate and the volume of withdrawal via the LAF (liquidity adjustment facility) for signs of a build-up in liquidity."        \par \par \uc2 INDIA-RUPEE/|LANGEN|SNI   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070923e39k0008a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
