\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asian high-yielders surge after Fed rate cut   \b0\par\par\uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2007\par 05:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 19 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso rose more than 1 percent against the dollar on Wednesday after a large Fed rate cut revived appetite for high-yielding assets and allayed concerns over global growth.   \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve slashed its benchmark rate to 4.75 percent from 5.25 percent, boosting stocks and high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on hopes that the monetary easing would shield the U.S. economy from the housing slump and financial market turmoil.   \par \par \uc2 The Aussie dollar <AUD=> posted its biggest daily gain in nine years after the Fed move. In emerging Asia, the rupiah <IDR=> and peso <PHP=> hit their highest levels in nearly six weeks.   \par \par \uc2 The rupiah touched 9,230 per dollar and the peso rose to 45.55 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> also gained nearly 1 percent, rising to about 3.4535 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "The Fed has paved the way for risk-taking by offshore investors," said Suresh Ramanathan, head of strategy at CIMB Bank in Kuala Lumpur.   \par \par \uc2 Ramanathan said there had been foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows into Malaysian bonds as well as short-term bills, with 3-month bill yields <0#MYBILLS=> down by about 3 basis points.   \par \par \uc2 "Most of the interest seems to be within one to three months, an indication that investors are positioning for further rate cuts by the Fed in the next three months," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The spotlight in Wednesday's trading seemed to be on yields. The dollar was the clear loser and high-yielders such as the New Zealand dollar <NZD=> gained.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen <JPY=> fell initially in an apparent sign that people were using it again as a cheap funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but recovered later after Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said Japanese rates would still be raised if the economy performed in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's view.   \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=>, whose overnight call rate target is now above U.S. overnight rates after the Fed rate cut, gained half a percent to a high of 925 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "The mix of rising risk appetite, improved growth confidence, a markedly weaker dollar, clean speculative positions and emerging offshore interest to re-enter short dollar/Asia should help dollar/Asia push lower in response,"  \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  said to clients.   \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said the rupiah, peso and ringgit were more sensitive to increased risk appetite, but the Singapore dollar <SGD=> would remain sensitive to sentiment about global growth prospects.   \par \par \uc2 While the won and Indian rupee <INR=IN> should also trade with a bias to appreciate, heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  and likely Bank of Korea resistance would offset their gains,  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said.   \par \par \uc2 South Korea's second vice finance minister, Lim Young-rok, said on Wednesday that policy makers would take action if the won rose high enough to hurt the economy.   \par \par \uc2 SOME DOUBTS   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts felt the Fed's hefty rate cut might eventually bring to the fore concerns over growth in the United States and therefore in regions dependent on demand there, such as Asia.   \par \par \uc2 "That is the initial reaction, that the Fed reflation will work, but we think it won't," said  \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  strategist Thio Chin Loo.   \par \par \uc2 Thio said she was bullish on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Singapore dollar in such an environment. The Singapore dollar <SGD=> rose half a percent to 1.5085 per U.S. dollar on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 "We are no longer in a carry environment strictly, as yields in U.S. dollar will lose attraction, the ECB is on hold and the Bank of Japan defers rate hikes.   \par \par \uc2 "Also, capital preservation is more important in a lower growth environment, and financing concerns dominate.   \par \par \uc2 "Countries with large current account deficits like the U.S., UK, and Australia have \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are most vulnerable compared to Asian economies," Thio said.   \par \par \uc2 With the Fed meeting out of the way, some analysts highlighted rising oil prices as a concern for Asia, which imports most of its crude requirements.   \par \par \uc2 Oil prices <CLc1> hit a new peak above $82 per barrel on the New York Mercantile Exchange overnight, boosted by concern over supplies and the weather as well as expectations that the Fed rate cut would boost economic growth and therefore demand for oil. [nN18466256].    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0800 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.90         114.84       -0.91\par  Sing dlr        1.5059         1.5161       +0.68\par  Taiwan dlr      33.090         33.110       +0.06\par  Korean won      926.50         930.60       +0.44\par  Baht             34.24          34.25       +0.03\par  Peso             45.57          46.13       +1.23\par  Rupiah         9235.00        9387.00       +1.65\par  Rupee            40.23          40.51       +0.71\par  Ringgit         3.4535         3.4875       +0.98\par  Yuan            7.5121         7.5261       +0.19\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22