\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-In praise of capital controls in Asia   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan   \par \uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2007\par 07:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 10 (Reuters) - It's not what the International Monetary Fund would espouse. But as Asia's biggest economies turn to capital controls to shield their markets from a torrent of foreign money, many feel policy makers are doing the right thing.   \par \par \uc2 One message rings out loud from India's and South Korea's efforts in the past week to restrict the flow of foreign funds: these central banks would gladly abandon free movement in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the liberty of setting interest rates at will. Economists sympathise with that stance. Until this week, when delinquencies in U.S. subprime mortgages drove a broad selling of risky assets in Asia and elsewhere in the world, most of the region was drowning in foreign money.   \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks were under fire from their export lobbies as \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied and from their political masters as domestic asset prices soared. They were running out of tools to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets and were unenthusiastic about raising interest rates for fear that would bring in more cash.   \par \par \uc2 So when South Korea and India announced in the past week that they would restrict the amount of overseas borrowings locals could bring home -- effectively closing a small part of their capital accounts -- there was barely a murmur from proponents of free market principles.   \par \par \uc2 The implication was clear. When it comes to the crunch, Asia would rather have an independent monetary policy, even if that means a fettered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Bank of Korea's surprise rate rise on Thursday, its second in as many months, proved this.   \par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Korea has regained greater control of monetary policy," said  \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  strategist Stewart Newnham.   \par \par \uc2 The capital controls should alleviate pressure on the won to appreciate by restricting the main domestic speculators, he said.   \par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Korea is thus faced with a more favourable trade-off in meeting its inflation and exchange rate objectives, which should reduce its policy dilemma," Newnham said.   \par \par \uc2 The speculative froth in won markets will subside. The won <KRW=> -- estimated to be the most overvalued in Asia in trade-weighted terms -- would be among the region's underperformers and Korean market rates could rise as more locals shift to borrowing onshore, he said.   \par \par \uc2 CONTROLS OVERDUE?   \par \par \uc2 Analysts reckon capital restrictions were overdue in India.   \par \par \uc2 Generous limits had allowed companies to raise huge amounts of external commercial borrowings (ECBs) at rates cheaper than domestic funds, which then undermined the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy and put heavy pressure on the rupee to appreciate.   \par \par \uc2 ECBs doubled to $16.1 billion in the year to March 2007 from the previous year.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> has risen 15 percent in a year and is close to hitting 40 per dollar, the stock market <.BSESN> has gained 60 percent since the end of 2005 and property prices all over the country have soared.   \par \par \uc2 Overnight rates crashed to near zero earlier this month, in spite of a 6 percent base policy rate and a hawkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The government has now said companies borrowing more than $20 million would have to spend the money overseas.   \par \par \uc2 Rajeev Malik,  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's economist, said India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was dealing with conflicting goals of a "gung-ho approach for attracting more capital inflows but resistance to further rupee appreciation".   \par \par \uc2 He reckoned the new rules would impact inflows and facilitate better handling of money market liquidity by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While the rupee could appreciate in the longer term, it was likely to fall to 42 per dollar by the year end, he said.   \par \par \uc2 GOOD CONTROLS, BAD CONTROLS   \par \par \uc2 One reason economists want to forgive governments in Asia that want to restrain their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is China's reluctance to let the yuan rise, despite a surging trade surplus.   \par \par \uc2 And because emerging Asia's biggest economy places greater priority on maintaining its pole position in export markets and on creating jobs, its neighbours would be ill-advised to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  become uncompetitively overvalued.   \par \par \uc2 To their credit, India and Korea have turned to capital controls only as a last resort, after having let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate, raising interest rates and trying to encourage local investors to take their money abroad. Both have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  extensively, with \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves up $36 billion in the first half of 2007 and Korea's up by nearly $12 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Neither has interfered much with foreign capital investment or other healthier funding flows coming into their countries.   \par \par \uc2 "It is very hard to regulate a capital account that has already been opened. Investors typically find loopholes and history shows that the measures tend to work only for a little while," said Rob Subbaraman, an economist with  \uc2 Lehman Brothers.\par \par \uc2 "There are various degrees of imposing restrictions and I think this is a mild form. And I wouldn't see them in isolation," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Lessons had been learnt from Thailand's botched efforts to block capital flows in December, Subbaraman said, and policy makers in Asia were being extra cautious.   \par \par \uc2 ASIA-ECONOMY/CONTROLS (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|J|SI|PHD|IF|SNI|SNS|AUF|ND   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070813e38a000b8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
