\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  not reviewing  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  bond cap now-finmin   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Unni Krishnan   \par \uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2007\par 09:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, market comments)   \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Aug 8 (Reuters) - India is not reviewing for now a 1.1 trillion rupee ($27 billion) limit on the sale of bonds used to absorb funds generated by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but will consider it if needed, the finance minister said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Outstanding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds will rise to more than 950 billion rupees after an auction on Wednesday -- which the Reserve Bank of India said in April was the threshold for a review -- and traders expect the cap to be raised as the rupee <INR=IN> trades near nine-year highs against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "You can't say a review is on," Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram told Reuters when asked if the finance ministry was reviewing the limit for Market Stabilisation Scheme (MSS) bonds.   \par \par \uc2 "We will review it if it's necessary," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI has bought billions of dollars to rein in the rupee, which, powered by foreign investment inflows, has risen about 10 percent against the dollar this year, causing policy headaches.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  releases rupees into the local money market, which adds to inflationary pressures. To remove, or sterilise, the cash, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March resumed sales of MSS bonds.   \par \par \uc2 At a July 31 policy review, the RBI increased reserve requirements for banks, a step which it expects to remove $4 billion from the money market, and on Tuesday the government announced restrictions on foreign borrowing by local firms.   \par \par \uc2 With the RBI wanting to contain inflation but also expected to avoid raising interest rates if it can -- higher rates would attract more foreign capital -- the market expects the MSS limit to be increased. It was last raised in April.   \par \par \uc2 "I think they will do it. The way things are going, it looks like (the RBI) is determined to suck out excess liquidity from the system," a senior dealer at a private sector bank said.   \par \par \uc2 Asia's third-largest economy grew by 9.4 percent in the 2006/07 fiscal year to March 31, its fastest pace in 18 years, but the growth was accompanied by a surge in prices.   \par \par \uc2 Annual inflation hit a two-year high of 6.7 percent in late January, but has been below 5 percent since June.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI raised interest rates five time in 2006/07, but has not raised them since late March. (Additional reporting by V. Ramakrishnan in MUMBAI) ($1=40.40 rupees)   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/FINMIN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070808e388000ve\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
