\par\par \b \uc2 India Bonds Gain On Reserve Norm Requirements; Rupee Firms   \b0\par\par\uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2007\par 14:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest      Change \par  USD/INR           40.42       -0.04 \par  7.49% 2017 Bond   7.88%       -3BPs \par  Call Rate         0.10       -5BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par    July            0.76       +0.02 . *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bond prices rose Thursday as banks bought securities to meet reserve requirements, but may come under pressure ahead of a federal government bond auction Friday, dealers said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 7.49% 2017-dated bond ended at INR97.35, higher than its close of INR97.15 Wednesday. Its yield fell by 3 basis points to 7.88%.   \par \par \uc2 "We are seeing good buying from banks to meet their statutory liquidity ratio requirements before the reporting day tomorrow," said a trader at a private bank.   \par \par \uc2 Indian banks need to maintain 25% of their deposits in gold, cash or approved securities and have to report the position to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  every alternate Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Market participants also took comfort from a recovery in local stocks, which boosted hopes of more inflows in to the country.   \par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's benchmark 30-stock Sensex gained 49.93 points, or 0.3%, to end at 14985.70.   \par \par \uc2 The inflows could partly offset the squeeze expected in money markets next week, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 Liquidity in the banking system is expected to tighten next week after the 50 basis point hike in the cash reserve ratio to 7% takes effect Saturday and the ceiling on the excess funds that RBI accepts via the reverse repo auctions is scrapped.   \par \par \uc2 "As long as capital flows are there in the market and the rupee is rising, the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and in turn release rupee funds back in to the system," said a dealer with a state-run bank.   \par \par \uc2 However, traders expect bond prices to retrace some of their gains Friday as investors may look for higher yields at a federal government bond auction.   \par \par \uc2 "With the auction coming up tomorrow, we could again see some selling as players are still unsure of how the liquidity situation pans out," the trader at the private bank said.   \par \par \uc2 The trader expects the 10-year yield to test 7.92% Friday.   \par \par \uc2 RBI will sell INR60 billion of the 7.99% 2017-dated bond and INR40 billion of 7.95% 2032-dated bond under the federal government's regular market borrowing plan.   \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Indian rupee rose against the U.S. dollar tracking the recovery in local stocks and gains by local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other emerging market economies against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to INR40.42 from its finish of INR40.46 Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 "Traders had been funding their dollar purchases at near zero overnight rates lately and with call rate likely to rise again next week, traders may start going short on the dollar," a trader at a private bank said.   \par \par \uc2 The trader expects the dollar to trade in a range of INR40.35-INR40.50 Friday.   \par \par \uc2 -By Neelabh Chaturvedi; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alpana Sarma [ 02-08-07 1245GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070802e382000vn\par }\page {\par\fs20{