\par\par \b \uc2 India cbank buying more bonds with  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves   \b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2007\par 10:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, July 20 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increased the percentage of its rising \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that are invested in foreign bonds, its half-yearly report on the reserves shows.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) invested $19 billion in overseas bonds, more than half of the $34 billion increase in its reserves for the six months to the end of March 2007.   \par \par \uc2 Deposits with other central banks, the Bank for International Settlements (BIS) and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) rose by $16 billion, while deposits with foreign commercial banks declined by $1 billion.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI said it held \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves worth $192 billion as at March 31, the end of the 2006/07 financial year. Of those, $53 billion were invested in sovereign bonds, $92 billion with other central banks, the BIS and IMF, and $47 billion were deposited with foreign commercial banks.   \par \par \uc2 The percentage of its reserves invested in sovereign bonds rose to 27.6 percent from 21.6 percent six months earlier.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI also held gold worth $6.7 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Reserves grew 20 percent in 2006/07, and have risen more than 12 percent since then to a record $215 billion in early July, which analysts say largely reflects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the strength of the rupee <INR=IN>.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are the fifth-largest holdings in Asia.   \par \par \uc2 India's booming economy -- it grew 9.4 percent in 2006/07 -- has attracted foreigner investors to its markets and industries, and their demand for rupees has pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to nine-year highs against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment rose to $17.7 billion in 2006/07 from $7.7 billion a year earlier, the RBI said.   \par \par \uc2 The report is available on the RBI's Web site:   {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rbi.org.in"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.rbi.org.in  }}}\par \par \uc2 INDIA-CBANK/RESERVES|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070720e37k000qv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
