\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Bonds Dn On Higher Inflation,Supply Fears; Rupee Gains   \b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2007\par 15:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest      Change \par  USD/INR           40.43       -0.06 \par  7.49% 2017 Bond   7.93%       +2BPs \par  Call Rate         0.50%        Unchanged \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par    June            0.75       -0.04 . *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bond prices closed lower Friday on rising Indian inflation and expectations that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will sell more bonds in order to keep liquidity in the system in check, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect bond prices to remain under pressure next week after the Reserve Bank of India said it will sell INR180 billion of bonds and treasury bills next week.   \par \par \uc2 "Given the size of the auctions scheduled for next week, the yield on the benchmark 7.49% 2017-dated bond may test 8.00% on Monday," said Manoj Swain, head of fixed-income trading at Standard Chartered Bank.   \par \par \uc2 The 7.49% 2017-dated bond Friday ended at INR96.99, down from INR97.11 at the previous close.   \par \par \uc2 Its yield rose two basis points to 7.93%.   \par \par \uc2 Higher government spending and the likelihood that in recent weeks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has raised the amount of funds in the banking system, fueling speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take measures to reduce the amount of cash in the system.   \par \par \uc2 "It's a wait and watch policy now as everyone is expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell bonds to absorb liquidity," a trader at a European bank said.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI said Friday it will sell INR90 billion of bonds July 20, as part of the government's regular market borrowing plan.   \par \par \uc2 It will also sell INR50 billion of bonds and INR40 billion of treasury bills July 18, part of which will be launched off its market stabilization scheme.   \par \par \uc2 Under the market stabilization scheme, the RBI sells bonds additional to the government's regular issuance calendar, aiming to absorb excess liquidity in the banking system.   \par \par \uc2 Higher-than-expected inflation data also prompted some market participants to trim their bond holdings Friday, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 Wholesale price index inflation for the week ended June 30 rose to 4.27% from 4.13% a week earlier. India's inflation rate rose for a second week in a row on the back of higher food prices, according to official data.   \par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires' poll of six economists had forecast inflation at 4.12%.   \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Indian rupee rose against the dollar on strong foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  presence capped its gains.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR40.43 Friday, down from INR40.49 at the previous close.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "The supply of dollars is still there in the market, whether in the form of investments in the booming stock market or direct investments," said L.V. Prasad, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 IndusInd Bank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although expected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may cap the rupee's rise in the coming session, he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has shown the resolve to protect the dollar and this trend is likely to continue," Prasad said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He expects the dollar to trade in a range INR40.40-INR40.50 in the coming session.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Neelabh Chaturvedi, Dow Jones Newswires; 91-22-22884212-18; neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com [ 13-07-07 1328GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070713e37d000mg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
