\par\par \b \uc2 India rupee strength to persist on flows-top dealer   \b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2007\par 13:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, June 21 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is likely to be rangebound through July and remain near a recent nine-year peak, buoyed by capital inflows into the surging economy, a top dealer at Standard Chartered said.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee would be underpinned by about $40 billion of capital inflows in the fiscal year ending March 2008, led by a spurt in equity-related investments, he said.   \par \par \uc2 "Capital flows are strong and are going to remain strong," Agam Gupta, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and derivatives trading, told Reuters in an interview this week.   \par \par \uc2 "I also see a lot of money coming in the form of FDI (foreign direct investment) and private equity," said Gupta, who started his career in 1995 at  \uc2 ANZ Grindlays.\uc2  Standard Chartered bought it in 2000.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has gained about 8.5 percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year and hit 40.28 per dollar <INR=IN> late last May, its highest since 1998. It has since been trading in a broad 40.50-41.25 range and ended at 40.71/72 on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 "40.50 is where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown definite resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and when it spikes to 41.25, you see very, very good exporter interest and a lot of capital inflows."   \par \par \uc2 Gupta said he saw the rupee between 40.50-41.50 through to August.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively against the rupee on a day-to-day basis but would move to stem strong appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.   \par \par \uc2 "For the first part of the year its focus was primarily on controlling inflation. Now that the headline number is trending down, there is a definite shift in focus, partly to exchange rates."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely suspected of selling rupees over to stem its rise. This has led to a surfeit of cash in the system, reflected in overnight interbank lending rates hovering near a recent 10-year low of 0.1 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "The market is getting slightly confused by what is happening in the call market and is starting to accept that call rates are going to remain volatile on both sides of the corridor," said Gupta, who expects call rates to rise to 6-8 percent next week.   \par \par \uc2 He said most Indian companies with sizeable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposures had hedged against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations but many small exporters were still overexposed.   \par \par \uc2 "A lot of the smaller exporters have been dumbfounded by this extreme move and I hope they start hedging whenever they get a good level because I think this range is here to stay for some time."   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-RUPEE/STANCHART|LANGEN|SNI|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070621e36l000ze\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
